## Context

Init never overwrites, so canonical doctrine drifts away from what a host installed. Upgrade closes the gap across four kinds of host artifact, each handled differently. Package-owned files, the methodology and, as a category of its own, the tool and hook reference, have one correct copy, the running server's, so the only question is whether to take it. The aide server entry is judged against the global launch, so a launch through the package runner is drift; a pinned project first moves its pin to a version the caller names, since an old server cannot know newer releases. AIDE's hook entries belong in the project's per-machine local Claude Code settings, never the shared committed file, and must work on this machine; upgrade adds, rewrites or moves only AIDE's own entries. The brain config belongs to the user and drifts three ways: required sections missing, shipped sections on an old template version, and a launcher in a legacy shape.

Three parties each do part of the work. The tool holds its version's canonical bytes and can write host files. The agent talks to the user but cannot be trusted to reproduce canonical text. Only the user can approve a change. Under the source tree's rule only install, sync and a consent-gated upgrade write host files, so every repair comes through here.

The brain repair order and why responses stay small are set in the brain provisioning spec, and the hooks' installed form in the install service spec. This spec covers only what upgrade decides inside those flows.

## Strategy

**Detecting drift and applying it are separate calls.** A detection call reads every category, writes nothing and returns status, counts and names only. Applying a change is a separate call that names one category. The user's decision sits between the two calls, so no single invocation can both find drift and act on it, and a detection pass has nothing to apply. The agent shows each category as its own decision; nothing upgrades the whole surface on one yes.

**For package-owned categories the category is the unit, and the tool does the writing.** On a confirmed category the tool writes every differing or missing file from canonical and returns a manifest; half a category would leave files at mismatched versions, and the agent never sees canonical bytes to paraphrase. The tool and hook reference is a category apart from the methodology docs, its drifted pages named one by one, so a yes to the docs never rewrites a reference page.

**The aide entry moves to global only after the package is installed.** On the user's yes the agent installs the package globally, and the tool rewrites the entry to the bare global command only once that install is confirmed, refusing otherwise: an entry naming a command the machine lacks leaves the next session with no aide server. Only that entry changes, with no machine-specific path, and a correct global entry is never drift. The brain launcher moves through the brain config flow.

**A pin move is the one-time transit, and the caller names its target.** The pinned server predates the switch to global and cannot perform it, so it first moves to the latest release, whose upgrade then offers the switch. The boot report knows the latest and passes it; only an exact published version is accepted. On a yes the tool changes only the version, keeping the command form and every other byte, since the agent never hand-edits the MCP config. The new pin runs in a new session.

**AIDE's hooks live only in the per-machine local settings, and upgrade moves only AIDE's own entries.** The shared file is committed, so a server location there is wrong on every other machine; each developer's run installs their own. A local hook is current only if it runs this machine's installed server directly, skipping the shim's half-second per call on Windows, and gives session-end time to record the end. A yes writes the local entries and strips AIDE's from the shared file: nothing registered twice, nothing of the user's touched.

**The brain config is written only with consent, only by insertion, and only into the template's own integration.** A yes inserts every missing section, touching nothing already there, in the file's line endings so diffs stay honest. The manifest lists markers, never template bytes, and is empty on a re-run, so an interrupted repair is safe to retry. The template is written for Obsidian: for any other integration the tool inserts nothing and names the sections to adapt, since Obsidian instructions would mislead every later reader.

**Inside the brain config, each outdated section is its own decision, guarded by its exact bytes.** Stale sections and a legacy launcher sit outside the category yes; each is shown as its own before-and-after change and applied alone. The apply carries a byte-exact fingerprint of the before text the user saw; any change since is refused with an instruction to re-fetch the diff, because a whitespace-tolerant match would overwrite an edit made after review. Only text matching a known outdated template version is offered, so customized sections never are.

## Good examples

Detection reports four differing methodology docs and two drifted reference pages, each page by name. The user approves the docs and declines the reference. The tool writes the four docs and returns their names; the agent reports them without ever having seen their content, and both reference pages stay as they were.

A brain config is missing two sections and has one stale section. The user approves the brain category. One call inserts both sections in the file's existing line endings and returns two markers. The stale section is offered separately as a small diff, the user says no, and it stays as it is. A second run of the brain category returns an empty manifest.

A project carries AIDE's hooks in its shared settings, run through the package runner, beside a formatter hook and a permissions block the team wrote, and has no local file. The user says yes. The local file gains AIDE's hooks running this machine's installed server, session-end with its longer budget; the shared file loses only AIDE's entries, the formatter hook and permissions byte-identical. A teammate who pulls the change runs aide and gets hooks for their own machine.

## Bad examples

A yes to the brain category that rewrites the config from the template "to bring it fully current," or whose manifest echoes each inserted block "so the agent can confirm it." Every section appears and the tests pass, but the user's wiring and edits are replaced without approval, or every repair response grows by the full template size.

A hooks repair that writes the local entries but leaves AIDE's in the shared file "so teammates keep working." On this machine every hook fires twice; on every other clone the hooks call a server location that exists only here. Its sibling failure reformats the team's formatter hook while removing AIDE's entries.

A pin move that writes a floating latest tag "so it never falls behind again," or a switch that writes the bare global command before the global install has succeeded. Today's session works and the tests pass, but the next one either resolves whatever was published that morning, with files that no longer match the running version, or finds no aide server to launch.

